Revision history for File-SOPS

{{$NEXT}}
  - A number that was only printed or interpolated (my $s = "$n") is still
    just a number when written, on every supported perl: a string form that
    is exactly what perl writes for the number is no longer taken for a
    string of its own. Before 5.36 this made +Inf, -Inf and NaN, and in some
    cases integers, fail with "states a string half of its own". A scalar
    that carries a different text beside its number is still refused.
  - A brace that is not a Go RE2 repetition ({,3}, {,}, { 1,3}, {a}, {01})
    in an encrypted_regex, unencrypted_regex or path_regex is now matched as a
    literal on every perl, as sops does; before, {,3} meant different things
    (a literal, a fatal error, or {0,3}) depending on the perl version.
